      "response": {
        "category": "correctness",
        "consequence": "On timeout, join() can leave flusher processes running instead of terminating them. This is a regression from single-process behavior, where terminate() always ran. It can leave orphaned processes holding Redis and Kafka resources during shutdown or rebalance.",
        "evidence_refs": [
          "source:new:src/sentry/spans/consumers/process/flusher.py@sha256:ef3d47bfffc0264ff758b0c4a3d1b82e89de0437418a1141c09ec7fae9477ec3",
          "target:target:sym:c933d3f76e383384363d3afc"
        ],
        "explanation": "The old join() always called terminate() on the single process after the wait loop. The new code iterates over several processes. When the deadline has already passed at the start of an iteration, it executes `break`. That skips the `process.terminate()` call for the current process and for every process after it. Example: with 2 flusher processes and a short timeout, the first process consumes the whole timeout. The second process is then never terminated and keeps running.",
        "lines": {
          "end_line": 347,
          "start_line": 338
        },
